    "narrative": "For this August 13th slate, **Brice Turang** earns our top designation with a score of 63.99. While he faces a challenging arm in Roki Sasaki, the projection model is high on his speed potential; Sasaki\u2019s delivery cadence creates a favorable environment for **Brice Turang** to swipe a bag, and his promotion to the #2 spot in the order provides the volume necessary to capitalize on his elite sprint speed\u2014our primary scarcity focus. Behind him, **Travis Bazzana** (53.06) and **Seiya Suzuki** (51.85) provide solid, if unspectacular, floor production. **Travis Bazzana** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Montero, whose 4.43 xERA helps inflate our second baseman's profile, while **Seiya Suzuki** retains value despite a tougher look against Cade Cavalli, largely bolstered by his projected #2 spot in the lineup.\n\nOur start-sit calculus was particularly difficult regarding **Josh Naylor** (55.46) and **Jose Caballero** (42.14). While **Josh Naylor** represents our strongest power threat, he is forced into a neutral-to-negative matchup against Fried, with the platoon disadvantage suppressing his ceiling. The most interesting bench decision involved the proximity of the scores between **Jose Caballero** and our depth options; **Jose Caballero** ultimately earned the nod despite a lackluster 42.14 score because he remains our premier speed anchor, and we simply cannot afford to leave stolen base upside on the pine even in a suboptimal matchup against Gilbert. \n\nRisk management remains paramount as we navigate a lineup that is still \"pending\" for **Seiya Suzuki** and **Brice Turang**. Should either player shift down in the order, their daily efficiency scores will fluctuate. We are currently playing without the support of **Corbin Carroll**, **James Wood**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who are either sidelined by injuries or impacted by off-days. With our bench thinning, we must remain disciplined with 